# 1 Two infrared problems from Lesson 60

## 1.1 Soft photons, Rutherford's pole

Electron-muon scattering's dσ/dΩ∝1/t² with t=−4p²sin²(θ/2) produces ∫dθ/θ³ near θ=0. Physically, a massless photon can be exchanged with arbitrarily small energy, deflecting a charge by an arbitrarily small angle. A photon mass λ would replace 1/t by 1/(t−λ²) and cut the integral off at θ∼λ/p. The divergence as λ→0 is infrared: it comes from small k, not from Lesson 65's large k.

## 1.2 Collinear fermions

In Compton scattering the internal electron in the u-channel has q_B²=u, and u→0 when the outgoing photon is collinear with the outgoing electron. That is not a soft photon exchanged in the t channel. It is a fermion line going on shell. The total-rate divergence is still infrared (small invariant masses, long-wavelength configurations), and it is cancelled by a different set of real-emission diagrams (a collinear extra photon). Lesson 60's mistake, treating these poles as Rutherford's, would have sent both problems to the same cancellation; they cancel separately, against different real processes.

### Worked example 1

Give a one-sentence test that distinguishes the two. If the singular line in the diagram is a photon, the pole is the Coulomb/soft-photon singularity; if it is a fermion, the pole is collinear.

# 2 Virtual infrared divergences

Attaching a virtual photon to an external charged line and taking that photon's energy to zero produces, after the loop integration, a factor log(E/λ) multiplying the tree amplitude, where E is a typical energy of the process. Ultraviolet divergences of the same diagram were absorbed in Lessons 67 and 68. The infrared logarithm is not absorbed into e or m: it is a warning that the exclusive amplitude, by itself, is not what a detector measures.

# 3 Real emission

The amplitude to emit one extra soft photon of momentum k, polarization ε, from a charged particle of momentum p is, to leading order in k,

$$ ℳ_soft = e (p · ε / p · k) ℳ_tree

Lesson 74 derives this. |ℳ_soft|² integrated over photon energies from λ to ΔE produces a logarithm log(ΔE/λ) with a coefficient that is the negative of the virtual logarithm of Section 2.

# 4 Bloch-Nordsieck

Adding the virtual correction to the real emission, both computed to the same order in α, cancels log λ. What remains is log(ΔE/E), a finite number that depends on the detector. A better detector (smaller ΔE) sees a different exclusive rate; the inclusive rate, defined with that ΔE, is finite and is the prediction.

### Worked example 2

Why is "the total cross section for e⁻μ⁻→e⁻μ⁻ with no other particles" not a QED observable? Because any apparatus has finite energy resolution, so it cannot exclude extra photons of energy below that resolution; insisting on a strictly two-body final state asks a question no experiment answers, and QED correctly refuses to give a finite answer to it.

